<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="HW6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 6</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, March 5</alert></p>


  <p>
    Before working on this homework, please read from Chapter 14 in the book the definition of a module homomorphim/isomorphism and the definition of a free module. (We will talk about them in class but you should get a head start, so that you can start to think about these problems.) 
  </p>
<exercises xml:id="hw6-ex">
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
        [From the book]: Chapter 12: 4.3, 4.12
        </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <p>
       [Problem A] Let <m>R</m> be a ring. Consider <m>R</m> as a module over itself. If <m>a\in R</m> is an element, show that <m>f\colon R\to R</m> given by <m>f(x)=ax</m> is a module homomorphism. Conversely, if <m>f\colon R\to R</m> is a ring homomorphism, show that there exists  <m>a\in R</m> such that <m>f(x)=ax</m> for all <m>x\in R</m>. 
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <p>
       [Problem B] Let <m>R</m> be a ring and <m>I</m> a nontrivial (<m>I\ne 0, I\ne R</m>) ideal of <m>R</m>. 
        </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Show that <m>I</m> is free as an <m>R</m>-module if and only if <m>I</m> is principal, generated by a non-zero divisor. 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Show that <m>R/I</m> is not free. 
            </p>
        </li>
      </ol>  
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
<statement>
    <p>
    [Problem C]    Let <m>  R</m>   be a ring. An <m>  R</m>-module is said to be <term> cyclic</term> if it can be generated by one element. If <m>  M</m>   is a cyclic <m>  R</m>  -module, show that <m>  M\cong R/I</m>   for some ideal <m>  I</m>. 
    </p>
</statement>
<hint>
    <p>
        Use FIT for modules. 
    </p>
</hint>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
    <p>
    [Problem C]   Let <m>  R</m>   be a ring and let <m>  M</m>   be an <m>  R</m>  -module. The <term> annihilator</term> of <m>  M</m>   is defined as 
 <me>  
\Ann(M)=\{x\in R\mid xM=0 \,,\, \, i.e.\,\,\,  xm=0  \,\, \forall\,  m\in M \}.
</me>
</p>
Prove: 
 <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
          <m>  \Ann(M)</m>   is an ideal of <m>  R</m>  .   
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
           If  <m>  I</m>   is an ideal, then <m> \Ann(R/I)=I</m>.  
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
          If <m>  M</m>   is a free <m>  R</m>  -module, then <m> \Ann(M)=0</m>.  
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
             If <m>  N</m>   is a submodule of <m>  M</m>  , then <m>\Ann(M)\subseteq\Ann(N)</m>  . 
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
             If <m>  M</m>   has a free submodule, then  <m>\Ann(M)=0</m>. 
        </p>
    </li>
<li>
    <p>
         If <m>  U</m>  , <m>  V</m>   are two <m>  R</m>-modules such that <m>  U\cong V</m>  , then <m>\Ann(U)=\Ann(V)</m>. 
    </p>
</li>

 </ol>
</statement>
</exercise>

</exercises>
</section>